data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 11, the **sync-over-async opt-in for the Cosmos DB provider is removed entirely**. The synchronous LINQ operators (`ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`) and `SaveChanges()` now throw `InvalidOperationException` unconditionally — there is no configuration to re-enable them.

## The fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

## Summary of changes needed

| .NET 10 (opt-in worked) | .NET 11 (must change) |
|---|---|
| `ToList()` | `await ToListAsync()` |
| `First()` / `Single()` | `await FirstAsync()` / `await SingleAsync()` |
| `Count()` / `Any()` | `await CountAsync()` / `await AnyAsync()` |
| `SaveChanges()` | `await SaveChangesAsync()` |

You'll also need to propagate `async/await` up through your call chain (controllers, services, etc.). The `Add()` call itself remains synchronous since it only tracks in memory — no I/O occurs until `SaveChangesAsync()`.
